---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { link } from "../lib/paths";

const BOARDS_URL = "https://espressif.github.io/arduino-esp32/package_esp32_index.json";
---

<Base
  title="Getting started with the ESP32"
  current="start"
  description="Set up an ESP32 from zero: the right USB cable, drivers for CP210x and CH340, Linux serial permissions, Web Serial in Chrome and Edge, and the Arduino IDE."
>
  <section class="view prose">
    <div class="stack tight">
      <h1 class="h2">Getting started</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        From a board in a bag to a board printing to your screen, in about fifteen minutes. Most first-day problems are
        a cable, a driver or a permission, and they are all covered here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#need">What you need</a>
      <a href="#board">Pick a board</a>
      <a href="#plug">Plug it in</a>
      <a href="#check">Check it talks</a>
      <a href="#ide">Arduino IDE</a>
      <a href="#upload">First upload</a>
      <a href="#next">What next</a>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="need">1. What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>An ESP32 dev board. The ESP32-DevKitC, ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 and ESP32-C3-DevKitM-1 are covered in detail on <a href={link("/boards/")}>Boards</a>, and most clones work the same way.</li>
      <li><strong>A USB data cable.</strong> Many cables in drawers are charge-only: the board lights up but no computer ever sees it. If nothing shows up, try another cable first.</li>
      <li>Chrome, Edge or Opera on a desktop computer for the browser tools. Firefox and Safari cannot talk to serial ports.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="board">2. Pick a board</h2>
    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Board</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>ESP32 (classic)</td><td>Most tutorials and libraries, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth Classic and BLE, touch pins.</td><td>GPIO 34 to 39 are input only. A few pins set the boot mode.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ESP32-S3</td><td>More pins and memory, native USB, cameras and displays.</td><td>Two USB ports on many boards. Serial output may need USB CDC On Boot.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ESP32-C3</td><td>Small and cheap, Wi-Fi and BLE, native USB.</td><td>Fewer pins, no touch pins and no Bluetooth Classic.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="plug">3. Plug it in</h2>
    <p>
      Look at the small chip next to the USB socket. It decides which driver you need. ESP32-S3 and ESP32-C3 boards that
      use the chip's own USB need no driver at all.
    </p>
    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Chip marking</th><th>Driver</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">CP2102, CP2104</td><td>Silicon Labs CP210x. Built into Linux; Windows usually fetches it automatically; otherwise install it from Silicon Labs.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">CH340, CH343, CH9102</td><td>WCH CH34x. Built into Linux and recent macOS; on Windows install it from WCH if the port does not appear.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">No chip (S3, C3)</td><td>Native USB. No driver on Windows 10 or 11, macOS or Linux.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="h3">Linux: permission to open the port</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Add yourself to the group that owns serial ports: <code>sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER</code> on Debian, Ubuntu and Fedora, or <code>uucp</code> instead of <code>dialout</code> on Arch.</li>
      <li>Log out and back in so the new group applies.</li>
      <li>On Ubuntu, if a CH340 board appears and vanishes at once, the braille display service has claimed it: <code>sudo apt remove brltty</code>.</li>
      <li>Chromium installed as a Snap needs serial access: <code>sudo snap connect chromium:raw-usb</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="check">4. Check it talks</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open the <a href={link("/bench/")}>Bench</a> and press Connect.</li>
      <li>Pick the port: <code>cu.usbserial</code> or <code>cu.usbmodem</code> on a Mac, <code>COM3</code> or similar on Windows, <code>ttyUSB0</code> or <code>ttyACM0</code> on Linux.</li>
      <li>Leave the speed at 115200 and press the board's EN or RST button. A few boot lines should appear, starting with <code>rst:0x1 (POWERON_RESET)</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Seeing those lines means the cable, driver and permissions all work. Want to try flashing before installing
      anything? The <a href={link("/detective/")}>Detective</a> and the <a href={link("/projects/probeharbor-probe/")}>ProbeHarbor probe</a> flash straight from the browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ide">5. Set up the Arduino IDE</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Install Arduino IDE 2 from arduino.cc.</li>
      <li>Open File, Preferences, and add this to Additional boards manager URLs:<br /><code class="wrapcode">{BOARDS_URL}</code></li>
      <li>Open Tools, Board, Boards Manager, search for <strong>esp32</strong> and install <strong>esp32 by Espressif Systems</strong>. The projects here are written for version 3.</li>
      <li>Pick your board under Tools, Board, esp32: <strong>ESP32 Dev Module</strong>, <strong>ESP32S3 Dev Module</strong> or <strong>ESP32C3 Dev Module</strong>.</li>
      <li>On an S3 or C3 plugged into its native USB port, set Tools, USB CDC On Boot to Enabled, or nothing will print.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="upload">6. First upload</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Close the bench tab or press Disconnect. Only one program can own a serial port at a time.</li>
      <li>Open the <a href={link("/projects/blink/")}>Blink and heartbeat</a> project, copy the sketch into the IDE and press Upload.</li>
      <li>If the upload stops at <code>Connecting...</code>, hold the BOOT button, tap EN, then let go of BOOT. Some boards need this every time.</li>
      <li>When it finishes, reconnect the bench at the project's speed and watch the heartbeat.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="next">7. What next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={link("/projects/wifi-scan/")}>Wi-Fi scanner</a> and <a href={link("/projects/i2c-scan/")}>I2C scanner</a> need no extra parts, or one sensor.</li>
      <li>Not sure what to build? Tick your parts on the <a href={link("/#drawer")}>library</a> page and let it pick.</li>
      <li>Something went wrong? Paste the output into the <a href={link("/errors/")}>error reference</a> or the bench's crash translator.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
